@

The subcommand is [[required]] because [[modules]] is a container command:
all its work happens in [[list]], [[view]], [[move]], [[create]] or
[[delete]], and a bare [[canvaslms modules]] has no meaningful
interpretation.
Requiring it lets argparse answer such a command line with the usage message
listing the alternatives, which is both the most informative answer and
the earliest one.
Without it, argparse happily accepts the incomplete command line and returns
arguments where [[func]] was never set, leaving the main program to notice
that nothing can be run (\cref{missing-subcommand}).

There is another way to make a container command safe, the one [[assignments]]
and [[submissions]] use: leave the subcommand optional, but set a default
[[func]] on the container parser so that the bare command means \enquote{run
[[list]]}.
The price is that every option of the default subcommand must be repeated on
the container parser, since it is that parser which parses them.
We don't pay it here, which leaves requiring the subcommand as the only way
[[modules]] can respond sensibly---the same choice as the other container
commands without defaults: [[pages]], [[discussions]], [[calendar]],
[[syllabus]] and [[cache]].

[[list]], [[view]], [[move]] and [[delete]] accept a regular expression for
selecting modules by name or Canvas ID; [[move]] and [[delete]] require it
(\cref{sec:modules-move,sec:modules-delete}), [[create]] takes a module
name instead (\cref{sec:modules-create}).
This allows users to work with specific modules or groups of related modules.
Additionally, the [[view]] subcommand supports two optional flags that add
columns: [[--show-id]] includes Canvas item IDs and [[--show-position]]
includes each item's position within its module.
Both are useful for automation and scripting---IDs for constructing URLs,
positions for deciding where to place new items---but omitted by default for
cleaner, more readable output.

