\section{Producing a list of modules, [[modules_list_command]]}

The [[modules list]] subcommand provides a high-level overview of all modules
in a course.
For each module, we output metadata that helps users understand course
structure: when modules unlock, whether students must complete items in order,
and how many items each module contains.

The function~[[modules_list_command]] processes the command-line arguments and
outputs one row per module in CSV format.
<<functions>>=
def modules_list_command(config, canvas, args):
  output = csv.writer(sys.stdout, delimiter=args.delimiter)
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
  
  for course in course_list:
    modules = filter_modules(course, args.regex)
    for module in modules:
      <<write module information to output>>
@

\section{Viewing modules and their contents, [[modules_view_command]]}

The [[modules view]] subcommand provides detailed information about module
contents.
Unlike [[modules list]] which shows module-level metadata, [[modules view]]
enumerates every item within matching modules, including their type (Assignment,
Page, File, etc.), title, and completion requirements.

We must handle several edge cases:
modules with no items, API errors when fetching items, and optional inclusion
of Canvas IDs based on the [[--show-id]] flag.
<<functions>>=
def modules_view_command(config, canvas, args):
  output = csv.writer(sys.stdout, delimiter=args.delimiter)
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)

  for course in course_list:
    modules = filter_modules(course, args.regex)
    for module in modules:
      <<process module items with error handling>>
@

Canvas modules can be in various states:
some may contain no items (empty modules used as headers), while others may
have permissions or API issues preventing item access.
We handle these cases gracefully to ensure users see all matching modules, even
if we cannot retrieve their contents.

For empty modules, we output a row indicating \enquote{No items}.
For modules where item retrieval fails (due to API errors, permissions, or
network issues), we output an \enquote{Error loading items} message.
This approach ensures the user knows which modules exist and that we attempted
to process them, even when complete information is unavailable.

The bare [[except]] clause catches all exceptions because the Canvas API can
raise various exception types (network errors, permission errors, malformed
responses), and we want robust output rather than a failed command.

<<process module items with error handling>>=
try:
  items = list_module_items(module)
  if not items:
    message = "No items"
    <<write placeholder row for module>>
  else:
    for item in items:
      <<write module item information to output>>
except:
  message = "Error loading items"
  <<write placeholder row for module>>
@

Both placeholder rows must have exactly as many columns as an item row, or
the output stops being a table: a consumer doing [[cut -f]] would read the
message as a title in one row and as a type in the next.
The item row has two optional columns, so the placeholder has to pad for
each flag that is on.
Producing both placeholders from one chunk keeps the padding in one place;
the message sits in the item-type column, where a reader scanning the
table expects to learn what kind of row this is.
<<write placeholder row for module>>=
row = [course.course_code, module.name]
if args.show_position:
  row.append("")
row += [message, ""]
if args.show_id:
  row.append("")
row.append("")
output.writerow(row)
@

\subsection{Module metadata output format}

For the [[modules list]] command, we output five pieces of information per
module:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Course code
\item Module name
\item Unlock date (when the module becomes available to students)
\item Progress mode (explained below)
\item Item count (number of items in the module)
\end{enumerate}

<<write module information to output>>=
# Get module items count
try:
  items = list_module_items(module)
  item_count = len(items)
except:
  item_count = 0

<<format progress mode descriptively>>

output.writerow([
  course.course_code,
  module.name,
  module.unlock_at,
  progress_mode,
  item_count
])
@

The Canvas API provides a boolean [[require_sequential_progress]] field
indicating whether students must complete module items in order.
However, outputting [[True]]/[[False]] values would require users to consult
documentation to understand their meaning.
Instead, we output self-explanatory strings: ``sequential'' when items must be
completed in order, and ``any-order'' when students can complete items in any
sequence.
This follows the Unix philosophy of making output immediately understandable.

<<format progress mode descriptively>>=
sequential = bool(module.require_sequential_progress)
progress_mode = "sequential" if sequential else "any-order"
@

\subsection{Module item output format}

For the [[modules view]] command, we output information about each item within
matching modules.
The output includes: course code, module name, optionally the item's position
(when [[--show-position]] is specified), item type, item title, optionally the
Canvas ID (when [[--show-id]] is specified), and any completion requirements.

Completion requirements specify what students must do to mark an item complete:
they might need to view the item, submit an assignment, or achieve a minimum
score.
We format these requirements in a human-readable way, including threshold scores
when applicable.

Canvas item IDs are useful for automation (e.g., constructing URLs or making
API calls) but add visual clutter for humans reading the output.
Therefore, we make ID output optional via the [[--show-id]] flag, defaulting to
cleaner output without IDs.

The position is Canvas's own 1-based ordinal of the item within its module,
the number one writes in front matter to place an item (\cref{sec:module-membership}).
Canvas already returns items in position order, so for reading the output
the number is redundant; it earns its column only when the reader is about
to reorder.
We therefore gate it behind [[--show-position]], and for the same reason as
for IDs: every column added by default shifts the fields that existing
[[cut -f]] scripts rely on.

<<write module item information to output>>=
<<extract completion requirement>>
<<build item output row conditionally>>
@

<<extract completion requirement>>=
completion_req = ""
if item.completion_requirement:
  req = item.completion_requirement
  if 'type' in req:
    completion_req = req['type']
    if 'min_score' in req:
      completion_req += f" (min score: {req['min_score']})"
@

We build the output row incrementally.
The base row always includes course, module, item type, and item title.
If the user requested positions via [[--show-position]], we insert the
position right after the module name, where it reads as \enquote{item
number $n$ of this module}; if the user requested IDs via [[--show-id]], we
insert the Canvas ID before the completion requirement.
This approach maintains consistent column ordering while allowing optional
fields.

<<build item output row conditionally>>=
row = [course.course_code, module.name]

if args.show_position:
  row.append(item.position if item.position is not None else "")

row += [
  item.type or "Unknown",
  item.title or "No title"
]

if args.show_id:
  if item.content_id is not None:
    row.append(item.content_id)
  elif item.id is not None:
    row.append(item.id)
  else:
    row.append("")

row.append(completion_req)
output.writerow(row)
@

Let's verify the column layout.
The property we care about is that every row of one invocation has the same
number of columns, whatever combination of flags is given, and that the
position lands right after the module name.
We build a course with one module and let the test choose the module's items
(or make item retrieval fail), then parse the TSV that the command prints.
<<test functions>>=
def run_modules_view(items, capsys, show_id=False, show_position=False):
  """Run modules view on one module and return the parsed TSV rows."""
  course = MagicMock()
  course.course_code = "CODE"
  module = MagicMock()
  module.name = "Week 1"
  module.id = 11
  if isinstance(items, Exception):
    module.get_module_items.side_effect = items
  else:
    module.get_module_items.return_value = items
  course.get_modules.return_value = [module]
  args = argparse.Namespace(regex=".*", delimiter="\t",
                            show_id=show_id, show_position=show_position)
  with patch("canvaslms.cli.modules.courses.process_course_option",
             return_value=[course]):
    modules_view_command({}, MagicMock(), args)
  return list(csv.reader(io.StringIO(capsys.readouterr().out),
                         delimiter="\t"))

def make_module_item(position, title="Item", item_type="Page"):
  """Create a module item mock with the attributes the CLI reads."""
  item = MagicMock()
  item.type = item_type
  item.title = title
  item.position = position
  item.content_id = None
  item.id = 5
  item.completion_requirement = None
  return item

def test_view_position_column_after_module_name(capsys):
  rows = run_modules_view([make_module_item(2)], capsys, show_position=True)
  assert rows == [["CODE", "Week 1", "2", "Page", "Item", ""]]

def test_view_without_position_flag_keeps_old_layout(capsys):
  rows = run_modules_view([make_module_item(2)], capsys)
  assert rows == [["CODE", "Week 1", "Page", "Item", ""]]

@pytest.mark.parametrize("show_id", [False, True])
@pytest.mark.parametrize("show_position", [False, True])
def test_view_placeholder_rows_match_item_width(capsys, show_id, show_position):
  item_rows = run_modules_view([make_module_item(1)], capsys,
                               show_id=show_id, show_position=show_position)
  empty_rows = run_modules_view([], capsys,
                                show_id=show_id, show_position=show_position)
  error_rows = run_modules_view(RuntimeError("boom"), capsys,
                                show_id=show_id, show_position=show_position)
  width = len(item_rows[0])
  assert [len(r) for r in empty_rows] == [width]
  assert [len(r) for r in error_rows] == [width]
  assert "No items" in empty_rows[0]
  assert "Error loading items" in error_rows[0]
@


\section{Moving an item within a module, [[modules_move_command]]}
\label{sec:modules-move}

The [[pages]], [[assignments]] and [[quizzes]] commands can place the items
they manage at a position in a module through the [[modules]] front matter
(\cref{sec:module-positions}), but that is the item's own view of the
world: it requires exporting the item, editing a file and pushing it back,
and it only reaches the kinds of item those commands manage.
A module also holds sub-headers, files, external URLs and tools, and an
instructor reordering a module wants to say \enquote{put that one third}
without round-tripping content.
[[modules move]] is that module-centric view:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms modules move -c prgi25 "^Week 1$" -i "^Lab 1" -p 3
\end{minted}

The module regex is required rather than defaulting to [[.*]] as it does for
[[list]] and [[view]]: listing every module is a harmless default, moving an
item in every module is not.
The item is matched by title or Canvas ID, like modules are, and the match
must be unique within each module, because a position is a single slot and
moving several items \enquote{to position~3} would only mean something
arbitrary.
A module with no matching item is reported and skipped rather than being an
error, so that a pattern can be applied across several modules of which only
some contain the item.
<<functions>>=
def modules_move_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Move an item to a 1-based position within each matching module."""
  if args.position < 1:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1, "Position must be 1 or greater")
  item_regex = re.compile(args.item)
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
  moved = []
  for course in course_list:
    for module in filter_modules(course, args.regex):
      <<move the matching item in [[module]]>>
  print_module_changes([], [], moved)
@

An item already at the requested position is left alone, so that repeating
a command is harmless; the move itself is [[move_module_item]]
(\cref{sec:module-membership}), the same function the front-matter path
uses, so both invalidate the module's cached items the same way.
<<move the matching item in [[module]]>>=
matches = [item for item in list_module_items(module)
           if item_regex.search(item.title or "")
           or item_regex.search(str(item.id))]
if not matches:
  canvaslms.cli.warn(
      f"No item matching '{args.item}' in module {module.name}")
  continue
if len(matches) > 1:
  titles = ", ".join(repr(item.title) for item in matches)
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Several items match '{args.item}' in module "
                       f"{module.name}: {titles}")
item = matches[0]
if item.position == args.position:
  continue
try:
  move_module_item(module, item, args.position)
  moved.append(module.name)
except Exception as e:
  canvaslms.cli.warn(f"Could not move '{item.title}' in {module.name}: {e}")
@

Let's verify the three outcomes per module---moved, already there, not
found---and that an ambiguous pattern stops the command.
<<test functions>>=
def run_modules_move(items, item_regex, position):
  """Run modules move on one module and return (module, captured stderr)."""
  module = make_cached_module("Week 1", 11, items)
  course = MagicMock()
  course.course_code = "CODE"
  course.get_modules.return_value = [module]
  args = argparse.Namespace(regex="^Week 1$", item=item_regex,
                            position=position)
  with patch("canvaslms.cli.modules.courses.process_course_option",
             return_value=[course]):
    modules_move_command({}, MagicMock(), args)
  return module

def test_move_edits_unique_match(capsys):
  lab = make_module_item(5, title="Lab 1")
  module = run_modules_move([make_module_item(1, title="Intro"), lab],
                            "^Lab", 2)
  lab.edit.assert_called_once_with(module_item={'position': 2})
  assert module.module_item_all_fetched is None
  assert "Moved within modules: Week 1" in capsys.readouterr().err

def test_move_leaves_item_already_in_place(capsys):
  lab = make_module_item(2, title="Lab 1")
  run_modules_move([lab], "^Lab", 2)
  lab.edit.assert_not_called()
  assert "Moved" not in capsys.readouterr().err

def test_move_skips_module_without_match():
  intro = make_module_item(1, title="Intro")
  run_modules_move([intro], "^Lab", 2)
  intro.edit.assert_not_called()

def test_move_refuses_ambiguous_match():
  items = [make_module_item(1, title="Lab 1"),
           make_module_item(2, title="Lab 2")]
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
    run_modules_move(items, "^Lab", 3)
  for item in items:
    item.edit.assert_not_called()

def test_move_rejects_position_below_one():
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
    run_modules_move([make_module_item(1, title="Lab 1")], "^Lab", 0)
