\section{Creating modules, [[modules_create_command]]}
\label{sec:modules-create}

Placing items in modules presupposes that the modules exist, and a course
set up from files (\cref{sec:module-positions}) wants that step scripted
too.
[[modules create]] creates one module per selected course:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms modules create -c prgi25 "Week 1" --position 1 --unlock-at 2026-08-24 --publish
\end{minted}
Canvas is happy to create several modules with the same name, which in a
script is almost always a re-run rather than an intention, so we refuse a
name that already exists in the course unless [[--allow-duplicate]] says
otherwise.
The new module is printed as a [[modules list]] row, so that a script can
read back what it created in the format it already parses.

The course's module list is cached across runs (\cref{sec:caching-module-items}
describes the mechanism for items; modules are cached the same way on the
[[Course]] object), so after creating or deleting a module we forget the
course's cached modules, exactly as [[invalidate_module_items]] does for a
module's items.
<<functions>>=
def invalidate_course_modules(course):
  """Forget the cached modules of `course` so the next access refetches."""
  if hasattr(course, 'module_cache'):
    course.module_cache.clear()
  if hasattr(course, 'module_all_fetched'):
    course.module_all_fetched = None

def modules_create_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Create a module in each selected course and print it as a list row."""
  output = csv.writer(sys.stdout, delimiter=args.delimiter)
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
  for course in course_list:
    <<refuse to duplicate an existing module name>>
    <<build the module attributes from the options>>
    module = canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_module_attrs(
        course.create_module(module_attrs))
    invalidate_course_modules(course)
    <<publish the new module if requested>>
    <<write module information to output>>
@

<<refuse to duplicate an existing module name>>=
existing = [m for m in course.get_modules()
            if canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_module_attrs(m).name == args.name]
if existing and not args.allow_duplicate:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Module '{args.name}' already exists in "
                       f"{course.course_code}; use --allow-duplicate to add "
                       "another")
@

Only the options the user gave are sent, so Canvas applies its own
defaults for the rest (appended last, locked never, any order).
The unlock date goes through [[parse_date]] like the assignment dates do, so
the same human-readable forms work here.
<<build the module attributes from the options>>=
module_attrs = {'name': args.name}
if args.position is not None:
  module_attrs['position'] = args.position
if args.unlock_at:
  module_attrs['unlock_at'] = canvaslms.cli.utils.parse_date(args.unlock_at)
if args.sequential:
  module_attrs['require_sequential_progress'] = True
@

Canvas creates modules unpublished and accepts [[published]] only on
update, hence the second request.
<<publish the new module if requested>>=
if args.publish:
  module.edit(module={'published': True})
@

<<add create arguments>>=
create_parser.add_argument("name",
  help="Name of the new module")
courses.add_course_option(create_parser, required=True)
create_parser.add_argument("-p", "--position", type=int,
  help="1-based position among the course's modules (default: last)")
create_parser.add_argument("--unlock-at",
  help="When the module unlocks for students, e.g. '2026-08-24 08:00'")
create_parser.add_argument("--sequential", action="store_true",
  help="Require students to complete the items in order")
create_parser.add_argument("--publish", action="store_true",
  help="Publish the module right away (default: unpublished)")
create_parser.add_argument("--allow-duplicate", action="store_true",
  help="Create the module even if one with the same name exists")
@

Let's verify that the module is created from the options, that the cached
module list is forgotten, and that a duplicate name is refused.
<<test functions>>=
def run_modules_create(existing_names, capsys, **options):
  """Run modules create on one course; return (course, created module)."""
  course = MagicMock()
  course.course_code = "CODE"
  mods = []
  for name in existing_names:
    m = MagicMock(); m.name = name; mods.append(m)
  course.get_modules.return_value = mods
  course.module_cache = {1: (MagicMock(), {})}
  course.module_all_fetched = datetime.now()
  created = MagicMock()
  created.name = options.get("name", "New")
  created.unlock_at = None
  created.require_sequential_progress = False
