\section{Deleting modules, [[modules_delete_command]]}
\label{sec:modules-delete}

Deleting a module removes the module and its items from the course
structure; the pages, assignments and files the items pointed to stay in
the course.
That makes it less destructive than it sounds, but a regex can match more
than the user had in mind, so we follow [[quizzes delete]]: show what will
be deleted, ask, and let [[--force]] skip the question for scripts.
Unlike [[quizzes delete]] we do accept several matches---tearing down a
whole set of [[Week .*]] modules is the common case---which is why the
listing and the question come before any deletion.
<<functions>>=
def modules_delete_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Delete the modules matching a regex, after confirmation."""
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
  targets = [(course, module)
             for course in course_list
             for module in filter_modules(course, args.regex)]
  if not targets:
    raise canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError(f"No modules match '{args.regex}'")
  <<list the modules about to be deleted>>
  <<ask for confirmation unless forced>>
  for course, module in targets:
    module.delete()
    invalidate_course_modules(course)
    print(f"Deleted module '{module.name}' in {course.course_code}",
          file=sys.stderr)
@

