\subsection{Module specifications: patterns with optional positions}
\label{sec:module-positions}

A [[modules]] list of plain regex strings can say \emph{which} modules an
item belongs in, but nothing about \emph{where} in each module it goes.
Canvas keeps every module's items in order and exposes that order as a
1-based [[position]]; adding an item without one appends it.
That is fine for a first import, but a course whose pages live in files
(one file per page, pushed one at a time) needs each file to carry the
page's place in the module, or the module order would depend on the order
in which files happen to be pushed.

So a [[modules]] entry may also be a mapping that pairs a pattern with a
position:
\begin{verbatim}
modules:
- ^Week 1$
- module: ^Week 2$
  position: 3
\end{verbatim}
The first entry is the familiar string form: the item must be in the
modules matching it, wherever it already is or at the end if new.
The second says the item must be the third item of every module matching
[[^Week 2$]].
We chose a mapping over some inline syntax such as [[^Week 2$@3]] because
YAML parses it for us, the position stays an integer rather than a string
to be dissected, and the form has room to grow (a later
[[after: <pattern>]] would not change the string form).
The key is named [[module]] to mirror the [[-M]]/[[--module]] option, whose
value is the same kind of pattern.

The semantics of a missing position are the important design decision here:
a spec without a position leaves the item where it is, it never moves it to
the end.
This is what makes round trips safe---a file exported from Canvas and
pushed back unchanged must not reorder anything---and it is why the mapping
form with no [[position]] is allowed at all: it means the same as the
string, so exporters can always write mappings.
When several specs match the same module, the last one that carries a
position decides; when one pattern matches several modules, the item gets
that position in each of them.

We normalize the two forms into one shape, a list of
[[(pattern, position)]] pairs, so the rest of the code never has to ask
which spelling the user chose.
Anything else is a [[ValueError]] naming the offending entry, because the
alternative---silently skipping an entry---would leave the item in the
wrong module with no hint why.
<<functions>>=
def normalize_module_specs(specs):
  """Return module specs as a list of (pattern, position) pairs.

  Each entry of `specs` is either a regex string or a mapping with the key
  `module` (a regex) and an optional `position` (an integer >= 1). A missing
  position is returned as None; `specs` itself may be None, meaning no
  modules. Raises ValueError for anything else.
  """
  if specs is None:
    return []
  if isinstance(specs, str) or not isinstance(specs, (list, tuple)):
    raise ValueError(f"modules must be a list of patterns, got {specs!r}")
  normalized = []
  for spec in specs:
    <<normalize one module spec>>
  return normalized
@

A string is a pattern; a mapping must have a string [[module]] and nothing
but [[module]] and [[position]].
Rejecting unknown keys matters because a typo such as [[postion: 3]] would
otherwise be accepted as \enquote{no position} and the item would quietly
stay put.
<<normalize one module spec>>=
if isinstance(spec, str):
  normalized.append((spec, None))
  continue
if not isinstance(spec, dict):
  raise ValueError(
      f"module spec must be a pattern or a mapping, got {spec!r}")
unknown = set(spec) - {"module", "position"}
if unknown:
  raise ValueError(f"module spec {spec!r} has unknown keys: "
                   f"{', '.join(sorted(unknown))}")
if not isinstance(spec.get("module"), str):
  raise ValueError(f"module spec {spec!r} needs a 'module' pattern")
position = spec.get("position")
if position is not None:
  <<check that [[position]] is a positive integer>>
normalized.append((spec["module"], position))
@

Canvas positions start at 1, so zero and negatives are errors rather than
\enquote{first}.
We must also rule out booleans explicitly: [[bool]] is a subclass of [[int]]
in Python, and YAML reads [[position: yes]] as [[True]], which would
otherwise pass as position~1.
<<check that [[position]] is a positive integer>>=
if (isinstance(position, bool) or not isinstance(position, int)
    or position < 1):
  raise ValueError(
      f"module spec {spec!r}: position must be an integer >= 1")
@

The commands that read front matter validate it before they write anything
to Canvas, collecting error messages with [[content.validate_attributes]].
[[modules]] is not part of those schemas, so we offer the same shape of
interface for it: a function returning a list of messages, empty when the
specs are fine, that callers can append to their own list.
Validating up front matters because the module update runs \emph{after} the
content has been written; an error raised only then would tell the user the
edit failed when in fact only the placement did.
<<functions>>=
def validate_module_specs(specs):
  """Return error messages for module `specs`; empty when they are valid."""
  try:
    normalize_module_specs(specs)
  except ValueError as e:
    return [str(e)]
  return []
@

Let's verify both spellings normalize to the same shape and that each
malformed form is rejected with a message that names it.
<<test functions>>=
def test_normalize_accepts_strings_and_mappings():
  specs = ["^Week 1$", {"module": "^Week 2$", "position": 3},
           {"module": "^Week 3$"}]
  assert normalize_module_specs(specs) == [
      ("^Week 1$", None), ("^Week 2$", 3), ("^Week 3$", None)]

def test_normalize_treats_none_as_no_modules():
  assert normalize_module_specs(None) == []
  assert normalize_module_specs([]) == []

@pytest.mark.parametrize("bad", [
    "^Week 1$",                                   # not a list
    [3],                                          # neither str nor mapping
    [{"position": 2}],                            # missing module
    [{"module": 7}],                              # module not a pattern
    [{"module": "x", "postion": 2}],              # typo'd key
    [{"module": "x", "position": "2"}],           # position not an int
    [{"module": "x", "position": True}],          # YAML yes
    [{"module": "x", "position": 0}],             # Canvas starts at 1
])
