\subsection{Updating module membership based on patterns}

The [[update_item_modules]] function synchronizes an item's module membership
with a list of module specs (\cref{sec:module-positions}).
The semantics are:
\begin{description}
\item[Empty list] Remove the item from all modules
\item[Non-empty list] The item should be in exactly those modules matching
  at least one pattern---add to matching modules, remove from non-matching
\item[Spec with a position] Additionally, the item should sit at that
  position in each module the spec matches---created there if new, moved
  there if present elsewhere
\end{description}

This provides a declarative interface: the user specifies the desired state,
and we compute the necessary additions, removals and moves.
Position is deliberately the only thing about ordering the user states: Canvas
renumbers the other items itself when one is inserted or moved, so
\enquote{be item~3} is a complete instruction, whereas anything relative to
siblings would need us to know and manage the whole module.

<<functions>>=
def update_item_modules(course, item_type, item_id, module_specs):
  """Update module membership for an item based on module specs.

  Args:
      course: Canvas course object
      item_type: 'Assignment', 'Quiz' or 'Page'
      item_id: For Assignment and Quiz: the numeric ID,
               For Page: page.url (string slug)
      module_specs: List of regex patterns or {module, position} mappings,
                    see normalize_module_specs. Empty list, or None,
                    means remove from all modules.

  Returns:
      Tuple of (added, removed, moved) as lists of module names

  Raises:
      ValueError: if module_specs is malformed.
  """
  <<normalize module specs>>
  <<find modules matching regex patterns>>
  <<compute and apply module membership changes>>
  return added, removed, moved
@

Our callers hand us [[module_specs]] straight out of YAML front matter, and
YAML draws its distinctions in a different place than we do.  A bare
[[modules:]] line with nothing after it parses to [[None]], not to the empty
list---yet to a human writing front matter the two say the same thing.  The
distinction that matters to us is whether the key is present at all, and the
callers have already settled that by testing [[if 'modules' in attributes]]
before calling us.  So by the time we are reached, the user has asked for
module membership to be set to whatever they wrote; writing nothing means
\enquote{no modules}, which is precisely the empty-list case above.

We normalise here rather than in each caller so that every call site---page,
assignment, and quiz editing alike---reads a bare key the same way, and so
that the two spellings of a spec (\cref{sec:module-positions}) arrive as the
same [[(pattern, position)]] pairs.  Without this, the loop in
[[<<find modules matching regex patterns>>]] would iterate over [[None]] and
raise a [[TypeError]].  Malformed specs still raise here, as a
[[ValueError]]; the callers are expected to have run
[[validate_module_specs]] before writing any content, so that this raise is
a safety net rather than the user's first notice.

<<normalize module specs>>=
module_specs = normalize_module_specs(module_specs)
@

First, we compile all regex patterns and find which modules match.
We match against both module name and Canvas ID, consistent with
[[filter_modules]].
Alongside the set of matching modules we record, per module, the position
the user asked for; a later spec overwrites an earlier one, which gives the
\enquote{last position wins} rule, and a spec without a position leaves no
entry, which is how \enquote{no position means don't move} falls out.
<<find modules matching regex patterns>>=
all_modules = [
    canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_module_attrs(module)
    for module in course.get_modules()
]

matching_module_ids = set()
desired_positions = {}
for pattern, position in module_specs:
  regex = re.compile(pattern)
  for module in all_modules:
    if regex.search(module.name) or regex.search(str(module.id)):
      matching_module_ids.add(module.id)
      if position is not None:
        desired_positions[module.id] = position
@

For each module, we determine the current state (is the item present, and
where?) and the desired state (does the module match a spec, and with which
position?).
Then we add, remove or move as needed.
<<compute and apply module membership changes>>=
added = []
removed = []
moved = []

for module in all_modules:
  <<find current module item if present>>
  should_be_in = module.id in matching_module_ids
  desired_position = desired_positions.get(module.id)
  <<add or remove item from module as needed>>
@

We search the module's items to find if our item is currently present.

For pages, we need to handle URL redirects. Canvas may store an old URL in the
module item that redirects to the canonical URL we're checking against. For
example, a page might have been added to a module with URL
\enquote{page-on-campus+zoom}, but Canvas later changed the canonical URL to
\enquote{page-on-zoom+campus}. The module item still stores the old URL, but
when we fetch the page using [[course.get_page()]], Canvas returns the page
with its new canonical URL.

Without handling this, the direct comparison [[item.page_url == item_id]] would
fail, causing the page to be added to the module again (creating duplicates).

To detect this, we fetch both the target page ([[item_id]]) and the module
item's page ([[item.page_url]]) and compare canonical [[url]] values. This adds
API calls, but only when the direct comparison fails.
<<find current module item if present>>=
current_item = None
canonical_page_url = None
if item_type == 'Page':
  try:
    canonical_page_url = course.get_page(item_id).url
  except Exception:
    canonical_page_url = item_id

try:
  for item in list_module_items(module):
    if item.type != item_type:
      continue
    if item_type in CONTENT_ID_ITEM_TYPES:
      if item.content_id == item_id:
        current_item = item
        break
    elif item_type == 'Page':
      if item.page_url:
        <<check if page item matches by resolving url>>
except Exception:
  pass
@

When comparing page URLs, we first try a direct match. If that fails, we fetch
the page to get its canonical URL and compare that. This handles the case where
Canvas has created a redirect from an old URL to a new one.
<<check if page item matches by resolving url>>=
if item.page_url == item_id:
  current_item = item
  break
# URLs don't match directly; compare canonical URLs to avoid duplicates.
try:
  resolved_page = course.get_page(item.page_url)
  if resolved_page.url == canonical_page_url:
    current_item = item
    break
except Exception:
  pass  # Page doesn't exist or can't be fetched
@

\subsubsection{Keeping the module item cache honest}

Module items are cached, and the cache outlives the process: the
[[Module]] objects returned by the cached [[course.get_modules()]] carry
their item list in [[module_item_cache]] and [[module_item_all_fetched]]
(\cref{sec:caching-module-items}), and that is written to disk between
runs with a refresh interval of days.
Nothing about the caching layer knows that [[create_module_item]] or
[[delete()]] changed the list.
Left alone, the next command---[[modules view]], or the membership check
for the next page in a loop---would read the stale list and, worse, stale
positions: Canvas renumbers all the siblings when an item is inserted,
removed or moved, so after any of these operations \emph{every} cached
position in the module is suspect.

The fix is local: after a successful mutation we drop the module's cached
items and reset its \enquote{all fetched} timestamp, which is exactly the
state of a module whose items have never been fetched, so the next access
refetches them.
The two [[hasattr]] guards let the helper run on plain [[Module]] objects
(and mocks) that never went through the caching decorator.
<<functions>>=
def invalidate_module_items(module):
  """Forget the cached items of `module` so the next access refetches."""
  if hasattr(module, 'module_item_cache'):
    module.module_item_cache.clear()
  if hasattr(module, 'module_item_all_fetched'):
    module.module_item_all_fetched = None
@

Moving an item is a [[PUT]] on the module item with the new position; Canvas
shifts the siblings to make room.
The [[canvasapi]] method [[ModuleItem.edit]] returns a fresh object and
leaves the one we called it on untouched, so after the call our
[[item.position]] is stale---one more reason to invalidate the module's
cached items rather than patch the object by hand.
We keep the move in a function of its own because the [[modules move]]
subcommand (\cref{sec:modules-move}) needs exactly the same two steps.
<<functions>>=
def move_module_item(module, item, position):
  """Move `item` to 1-based `position` within `module`."""
  item.edit(module_item={'position': position})
  invalidate_module_items(module)
@

Finally, we add, remove or move based on comparing current vs desired state.
When adding, we use [[create_module_item]] with the appropriate type and ID,
and with the position when the user gave one: Canvas accepts [[position]] on
creation and inserts the item there, which saves a second request and avoids
the item briefly appearing at the end.
When removing, we call [[delete()]] on the module item object.
When the item is already present and a position was asked for, we move it
only if it is not there already, so that pushing an unchanged file is a
no-op in Canvas as well as in our output.
Every mutation invalidates the module's cached items afterwards.
<<add or remove item from module as needed>>=
if should_be_in and current_item is None:
  # Add to module
  try:
    if item_type in CONTENT_ID_ITEM_TYPES:
      new_item = {'type': item_type, 'content_id': item_id}
    else:  # Page
      new_item = {'type': 'Page', 'page_url': item_id}
    if desired_position is not None:
      new_item['position'] = desired_position
    module.create_module_item(new_item)
    invalidate_module_items(module)
    added.append(module.name)
  except Exception:
    pass  # Silently skip if we can't add
elif not should_be_in and current_item is not None:
  # Remove from module
  try:
    current_item.delete()
    invalidate_module_items(module)
    removed.append(module.name)
  except Exception:
    pass  # Silently skip if we can't remove
elif should_be_in and desired_position is not None:
  <<move item to desired position>>
@

<<move item to desired position>>=
if current_item.position != desired_position:
  try:
    move_module_item(module, current_item, desired_position)
    moved.append(module.name)
  except Exception:
    pass  # Silently skip if we can't move
@

Let's verify the position handling: a new item is created at the requested
position (and without one when none was given), a present item is moved
only when its position differs, the last spec with a position wins, and a
move invalidates the cache just like the other mutations.
<<test functions>>=
def one_module_course(items, name="Week 1"):
  """Create a course mock with one cached module holding `items`."""
  module = make_cached_module(name, 11, items)
  course = MagicMock()
  course.get_modules.return_value = [module]
  return course, module

def test_create_passes_requested_position():
  course, module = one_module_course([])
  added, removed, moved = update_item_modules(
      course, 'Assignment', 42, [{"module": "^Week 1$", "position": 2}])
  module.create_module_item.assert_called_once_with(
      {'type': 'Assignment', 'content_id': 42, 'position': 2})
  assert (added, removed, moved) == (["Week 1"], [], [])

def test_create_without_position_omits_it():
  course, module = one_module_course([])
  update_item_modules(course, 'Page', 'intro', ["^Week 1$"])
  module.create_module_item.assert_called_once_with(
      {'type': 'Page', 'page_url': 'intro'})

def test_present_item_is_moved_when_position_differs():
  item = make_assignment_item(42, position=5)
  course, module = one_module_course([item])
  added, removed, moved = update_item_modules(
      course, 'Assignment', 42, [{"module": "^Week 1$", "position": 2}])
  item.edit.assert_called_once_with(module_item={'position': 2})
  assert (added, removed, moved) == ([], [], ["Week 1"])
  assert module.module_item_all_fetched is None

def test_present_item_at_position_is_left_alone():
  item = make_assignment_item(42, position=2)
  course, module = one_module_course([item])
  added, removed, moved = update_item_modules(
      course, 'Assignment', 42, [{"module": "^Week 1$", "position": 2}])
  item.edit.assert_not_called()
  assert (added, removed, moved) == ([], [], [])
  assert module.module_item_all_fetched is not None

def test_present_item_without_position_is_not_moved():
  item = make_assignment_item(42, position=5)
  course, module = one_module_course([item])
  update_item_modules(course, 'Assignment', 42, ["^Week 1$"])
  item.edit.assert_not_called()

def test_last_spec_with_position_wins():
  course, module = one_module_course([])
  update_item_modules(course, 'Assignment', 42, [
      {"module": "^Week", "position": 1},
      {"module": "^Week 1$", "position": 4},
      "^Week 1$"])
  module.create_module_item.assert_called_once_with(
      {'type': 'Assignment', 'content_id': 42, 'position': 4})

def test_update_rejects_malformed_specs():
  course, module = one_module_course([])
  with pytest.raises(ValueError):
    update_item_modules(course, 'Assignment', 42, [{"position": 1}])
  module.create_module_item.assert_not_called()
@

A Classic Quiz is matched and created as a [[Quiz]] item, and is not confused
with an assignment that happens to have the same numeric ID.
<<test functions>>=
def test_quiz_items_are_matched_by_type_and_content_id():
  assignment = make_assignment_item(42, position=1)
  quiz = make_module_item(2, title="Quiz", item_type="Quiz")
  quiz.content_id = 42
  course, module = one_module_course([assignment, quiz])
  assert get_item_module_positions(course, 'Quiz', 42) == [("Week 1", 2)]
  added, removed, moved = update_item_modules(
      course, 'Quiz', 42, [{"module": "^Week 1$", "position": 3}])
  quiz.edit.assert_called_once_with(module_item={'position': 3})
  assignment.edit.assert_not_called()

def test_quiz_items_are_created_as_quiz_type():
  course, module = one_module_course([])
  update_item_modules(course, 'Quiz', 42, ["^Week 1$"])
  module.create_module_item.assert_called_once_with(
